---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Safety | HerbGuide"
  description="Important safety notes, sourcing guidance, and plain-language boundaries for educational herb content."
  structuredData={[
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "WebPage",
      name: "Safety",
      description:
        "Important safety notes, sourcing guidance, and plain-language boundaries for educational herb content.",
      url: "https://herbguide.store/safety/",
    },
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are kitchen herbs always safe because they are foods?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Food-level use is often gentler, but quantity, concentration, product quality, current prescriptions, and personal context still matter.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does HerbGuide recommend herbs for specific concerns?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. HerbGuide explains traditional use, kitchen context, and safety language. It does not replace personal care guidance.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
  ]}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell narrow prose-block">
      <p class="eyebrow">Safety</p>
      <h1>How to read herb content responsibly</h1>
      <p>
        HerbGuide is an educational resource. It explains traditional context, general kitchen
        ideas, and research summaries, but it does not replace personal care guidance.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>We do not provide personal evaluations, care plans, or one-to-one guidance.</li>
        <li>Traditional uses are described as traditional uses, not as guaranteed outcomes.</li>
        <li>Pregnancy, nursing, prescription use, and chronic conditions all require extra caution.</li>
        <li>Readers should look for visible caution notes on specific herb pages.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Read carefully</p>
        <h2>Traditional use is not the same as a direct outcome claim</h2>
        <p>
          When a herb page says an ingredient is traditionally used in traditional herb writing to support qi,
          nourish blood, moisten dryness, or clear heat, that is a description of how the
          herb is discussed inside a traditional system. It is not a promise that the herb
          is appropriate, effective, or safe for every reader.
        </p>
        <p>
          That difference matters. Good educational herb content should help readers
          understand context, not push them into self-labeling.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Higher-risk situations</p>
        <h2>When extra caution matters most</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Pregnancy or trying to conceive</li>
          <li>Breastfeeding</li>
          <li>Prescription use</li>
          <li>Blood-thinning prescriptions or clotting concerns</li>
          <li>Autoimmune conditions, organ concerns, or complex history</li>
          <li>Children, older adults, or people with multiple ongoing health concerns</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>Chinese herbs safety guide for beginners</h2>
        <p>
          A food-style ingredient in soup is not the same thing as a concentrated extract,
          powdered supplement, tincture, or formula blend. Source, dose, preparation, and
          product quality all affect how a herb should be understood.
        </p>
        <p>
          HerbGuide will generally favor educational content around traditional food use,
          whole herbs, and careful terminology. That helps the tone stay practical and
          low-hype.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>Are Chinese herbs a good fit without personal guidance?</h2>
        <p>
          If discomfort is persistent, severe, worsening, or confusing, readers should not
          rely on blog content alone. The right next step may involve a qualified local
          professional depending on the situation.
        </p>
        <p>
          These pages can help readers ask better questions. They should not pretend to replace
          real evaluation.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Common questions</p>
        <h2>Kitchen-use safety, herb sourcing, and prescription caution</h2>
        <p>
          Many readers want to know whether kitchen-use herbs are automatically safe, how
          herb sourcing matters, and when prescription caution becomes important. The short
          answer is that context still matters, even for familiar ingredients.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Trust signal</p>
        <h2>Why clear safety language matters</h2>
        <p>
          Clear safety language helps readers understand that HerbGuide is educational
          first, not a shortcut to self-directed use. It also gives herb pages a stronger and
          more responsible foundation.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Reader questions</p>
        <h2>Herb safety FAQ</h2>
        <h3>Are kitchen herbs always safe because they are foods?</h3>
        <p>
          No. Food-level use is often gentler, but quantity, concentration, product quality,
          current prescriptions, and personal context still matter.
        </p>
        <h3>Should I use one herb page to decide what to take?</h3>
        <p>
          No. Herb pages are educational summaries. They are useful for learning terminology,
          common food uses, and caution notes, but not for personal decision-making.
        </p>
        <h3>Does HerbGuide recommend herbs for specific concerns?</h3>
        <p>
          No. HerbGuide explains traditional use, kitchen context, and safety language.
          It does not replace personal care guidance.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Keep exploring</p>
        <h2>Safety pages work best with herb pages</h2>
        <p>
          Once this page makes sense, go back to the
          <a href="/herbs/">Herb Library</a> and read caution sections on specific herbs
          like <a href="/herbs/goji-berry/">Goji Berry</a>,
          <a href="/herbs/licorice-root/">Licorice Root</a>, and
          <a href="/herbs/ginseng/">Ginseng</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          For gentler learning paths, continue to <a href="/glossary/">Glossary</a>,
          <a href="/topics/balanced-pantry-habits/"> Balanced Pantry Habits</a>, and
          <a href="/food-therapy/"> Kitchen Traditions</a>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
